          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. The surrounding qualification limits its extent but does not change the utterance from an avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal is explicitly framed by Cordelia as conforming to her existing filial bond. This invokes her obligation as Lear’s daughter rather than making a new promise. She later identifies the relevant duties as obedience, love, and honour. Lear does not accept this constrained account as an adequate response and tells her to amend it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mend your speech a little,\\nLest you may mar your fortunes.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The expression is a measured, propositionally explicit answer rather than a reflex-like discharge of affect. Cordelia describes difficulty verbalising her heart, but then deliberately states and qualifies her position.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression has a corrective and limiting function: Cordelia refuses Lear’s demand to rank her love competitively or claim total, unlimited devotion. “No more nor less” marks this boundary.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most, linking their answers to his distribution of bounty. After initially answering “Nothing” and being pressed to speak again, Cordelia avows love for him while defining it as the love appropriate to her filial bond rather than making the unlimited claim he seeks.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia makes no clear new promise. She invokes an existing daughter-to-father bond, under which she identifies duties to obey, love, and honour Lear. Lear rejects her formulation as inadequate and urges her to amend it, so there is no affirmative uptake of her bounded account.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text identifies the work as William Shakespeare’s The Tragedy of King Lear; the assessment uses only the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The excerpt supports judging the immediate avowal, its filial framing, Lear’s response, and Cordelia’s stated affection. It does not by itself settle broader relationship history, future conduct, or whether every element of the invoked bond is intended as a continuing commitment.\"}"
        }
